<p><a href="/contact-us?interest=Office%20space#Contact">Arrange an office viewing with your team brief</a>, or <a href="/contact-us?interest=Serviced%20offices#Contact">request an OnPoint serviced-office viewing</a>. Agree availability, required work and dates with leasing before planning a move.</p>$f4_4$, $f4_5$https://listings.blendproperty.co.za/uploads/listings/lst_import_030bec9330d4cea76339/633323cd-e715-42f8-8da8-d6778cc00679-optimized.webp$f4_5$, $f4_6$Office Viewing Checklist for Midrand Tenants$f4_6$, $f4_7$Plan an office viewing with checks for layout, access, parking, fit-out, connectivity and backup. Explore current Midpoint vacancies.$f4_7$, $f4_8$offices$f4_8$),
  ($f5_0$blog_office_floor_plan_20261019$f5_0$, $f5_1$office-floor-plan-fit-out-checklist-midrand$f5_1$, $f5_2$Office Floor Plans and Fit-Out: A Midrand Checklist$f5_2$, $f5_3$Use a current unit plan to map activities, test practical constraints and agree the fit-out scope, responsibilities and occupation programme.$f5_3$, $f5_4$<p>Use an office floor plan to test how the space will work for your team before deciding how much fit-out it needs. Published area alone does not establish practical desk capacity, accessibility, approval requirements or the cost of adapting the unit.</p>
<p>This planning checklist supports our <a href="/offices">Midrand office guide</a>. Use it after an initial shortlist and alongside the <a href="/blog/office-viewing-checklist-midrand">office viewing checklist</a>.</p>
<h2>Start with the correct plan and area basis</h2>
<p>Request a current, dimensioned plan for the exact unit, identifying its revision/date and existing versus proposed layout. Match the unit reference and priced area to the written quote. Ask how rentable area is measured and whether any shared or ancillary area is included.</p>
<p>The <a href="/vacancies/cmuz7chn0009up901ll35sgdh">Unit 2, 5 Weaver Avenue listing</a>, checked on 19 October 2026, publishes 2,405 m&sup2; at R110 per m&sup2; per month and describes an open-plan layout with a private outdoor area. That is a listing description, not a verified fit-out drawing, workplace capacity or confirmation that alterations are approved.</p>
<h2>Map activities before placing desks</h2>
<p>List the work your team needs to do: focused tasks, calls, private conversations, collaboration, client meetings, storage, deliveries and equipment use. Record typical and peak attendance, then place those activities on the actual plan.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Workstations:</strong> test furniture dimensions and the required circulation without assuming a standard desk count from the advertised area.</li>
<li><strong>Privacy and meetings:</strong> identify private rooms, noise-sensitive work, visitor waiting and routes that should not interrupt staff.</li>
<li><strong>Support space:</strong> locate storage, equipment, staff facilities and any delivery or waste route your operation needs.</li>
<li><strong>Fixed constraints:</strong> record columns, doors, stairs, service positions and features that cannot simply move with the furniture.</li>
<li><strong>Access and safety:</strong> ask the appropriate qualified advisers to assess accessibility, circulation, fire/escape requirements and the approvals applicable to the proposed use and work.</li>
</ul>
<p>A sketch is useful for a discussion, but it is not a certified capacity or compliance assessment. Confirm those requirements for the actual unit and proposed layout.</p>
<h2>Turn the layout into a scope of work</h2>
<p>Separate retained items from changes. List partitions, doors, furniture, cabling, connectivity, electrical work and any changes to building services. Confirm what the landlord provides, what the tenant supplies, who approves the design and which work requires specialist input.</p>
<p>Ask for cost, responsibility and timing beside each item. Include the dependencies: an occupation date is not credible if required design, approvals, installation or testing remain unresolved. Do not assume an advertised office is ready for your layout or that a contribution is available.</p>
<h2>Check service allocation against the plan</h2>
<p>Put critical equipment and connectivity needs on the drawing, then confirm the relevant supply and backup arrangements. Shared estate generator or backup-water features do not establish the allocation to your suite. Confirm supported equipment/circuits and current operating arrangements with leasing and the appropriate technical contacts.</p>
<p>If a serviced office is an alternative, test the actual suite and written package against the same activity map. The <a href="/blog/serviced-vs-conventional-offices-midrand">format comparison</a> and <a href="/blog/onpoint-serviced-office-package-checklist">OnPoint package checklist</a> identify the service and booking questions that a floor plan cannot answer.</p>
<h2>Connect the plan to the budget and live options</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/blog/office-rental-costs-midrand">office cost guide</a> to separate fit-out spending from recurring costs, and request a quote for the agreed scope. Browse <a href="/vacancies?sector=Office">current office vacancies</a> and the <a href="/offices#compare-spaces">live office comparison</a> for published options.</p>
